NoteRecall the probability model: outcomes, events and weights



An outcome is one result of the experiment; an event is a set of outcomes. The model is (Ω,ℱ,P)(\Omega,\mathcal F,P).
The power set 𝒫(Ω)=2Ω\mathcal P(\Omega)=2^\Omega is the collection of all subsets of Ω\Omega. We use it as the event collection for our finite examples. A sigma-algebra is an event collection containing Ω\Omega and closed under complements and countable unions; it consequently contains the empty set and countable intersections too. Thus the model can express “not,” “at least one” and “all” for the relevant lists of events.
Kolmogorov’s axioms say that probabilities are nonnegative, the whole space has probability 1, and probabilities add over countably many pairwise disjoint events. They do not require equal outcome weights. A random variable assigns a number to an outcome; its PMF collects the probability weights of all outcomes producing each numerical value.
See the definition boxes in Lecture 01.1 for small examples and the distinction between an event collection and a probability assignment.
